WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.860
Many queue systems use at-least-once delivery.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.840
That means duplicate processing should be expected, not treated as a rare failure.

3
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:19.100
Retries, consumer crashes, network failures, visibility timeouts, broker behavior, and manual replays can all cause a message to be processed more than once.

4
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:30.960
If the consumer sends money, changes permissions, emails customers, deletes files, provisions resources, or moves a workflow forward, duplicates can create real harm.

5
00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:37.080
Idempotent consumers produce the same safe result when the same message is processed more than once.

6
00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.900
The implementation depends on the business operation.

7
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:50.720
Use message IDs, idempotency keys, operation records, version checks, state transitions, conditional writes, or deduplication tables where appropriate.

8
00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.980
A payment worker might record an operation before charging.

9
00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:59.320
A file deletion worker might verify object version and state.

10
00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:05.180
A notification worker might deduplicate security alerts differently from marketing emails.

11
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:10.740
Ordering should not be assumed unless the platform and design explicitly guarantee it.

12
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:19.480
Even when ordering is promised within a partition or group, it may not hold across tenants, topics, queues, or failure recovery paths.

13
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:23.100
Consumers should check current state before applying changes.

14
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.880
A stale message should not overwrite a newer decision, roll back a workflow, or restore an old permission.

15
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:37.940
Replay protection should consider event IDs, timestamps, nonces, expiration windows, producer identity, and business state.

16
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.220
Deduplication scope matters.

17
00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:49.520
A globally unique message ID may be enough for one workflow, while another needs uniqueness by tenant, source, message type, operation, and business object.

18
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:55.700
Manual replay should be treated as a privileged operation because it can intentionally repeat old work.

19
00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:05.320
Build handlers as if duplicates, delays, reordering, and replay are normal operating conditions, because in many reliable queue designs they are.
